findings.body:
This PR flips `strict` from `true` to `false` in the app tsconfig:

::diff{file="ui-v2/tsconfig.app.json" lines="16-22"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This turns off `strictNullChecks`, `noImplicitAny`, `strictFunctionTypes` and the other strict flags for every file in `ui-v2/src`, not just the new components. Code that currently relies on null checks, such as `field.value?.[0]` and the many `?.` guards across the app, stops being checked. Nothing in the feature needs this. `FlowRunStateTriggerFields` follows the same pattern and already compiles under strict mode.

If one of the new files has a type error under strict mode, fix it locally, for example with a typed field name or a narrow cast. Don't loosen the compiler for the whole app. `tsconfig.node.json` still has `strict: true`, so the two configs now disagree as well.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/tsconfig.app.json" lines="19-19"}
		"strict": true,

`DeploymentStatusTriggerFields` and `FlowRunStateTriggerFields` choose the field based on posture: `after` for Proactive ("Stays in") and `expect` for Reactive ("Enters"). The two work components always write to `trigger.expect`:

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-pool-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="34-46"}

Compare the deployment version:

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/deployment-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="29-31"}

In the server's `EventTrigger`, `after` is empty, so `starts_after` returns False, and `expect=[prefect.work-pool.ready]`. A Proactive trigger set up that way fires when **no** `ready` event arrives within the window. The user picked "Stays in → Ready", but the trigger fires when the pool does *not* become ready, which is roughly the opposite. The same problem exists in `work-queue-status-trigger-fields.tsx` lines 34-46.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-pool-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="28-37"}
	const posture = useWatch<AutomationWizardSchema>({ name: "trigger.posture" });
	const statusFieldName =
		posture === "Proactive" ? "trigger.after" : "trigger.expect";

	return (
		<div className="space-y-4">
			<div className="flex items-end gap-4">
				<PostureSelect />
				<FormField
					control={form.control}
					name={statusFieldName}

One related problem: switching posture never clears the field you just left. If a user picks Ready while Reactive and then switches to Proactive, `expect=[ready]` is still there next to the new `after` value. That stale `expect` changes the trigger's meaning. Consider clearing the opposite field in `PostureSelect`'s `onValueChange`.

Every other threshold and within input uses `Number(e.target.value)`. This one uses `parseInt`:

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-pool-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="73-80"}

If the user clears the field, `parseInt("")` returns `NaN`, while `Number("")` returns `0`. With `NaN`, the `{...field}` value binding renders an odd value and zod reports "Expected number, received nan" instead of the min-1 message the other templates show. It also truncates `1.5` silently. The behavior differs depending on which template is selected.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-pool-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="78-78"}
									onChange={(e) => field.onChange(Number(e.target.value))}

The updated tests only check that the posture select and threshold input are visible:

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/trigger-step.test.tsx" lines="45-111"}

The logic this PR actually adds has no coverage:
- picking a status writes `["prefect.deployment.ready"]` and similar values;
- the status lands in `after` versus `expect` depending on posture, which is exactly where the work pool and work queue bug sits;
- the custom textarea's newline handling.

The PR checklist says unit tests were added, but there is nothing like `flow-run-state-trigger-fields.test.tsx` for the new components. Each component should get a small test that renders it inside a `useForm` provider, interacts with it, and asserts on `form.getValues("trigger")`.

The three status components differ only in their option list, and in whether they switch between `after` and `expect` (which already drifted, see the Proactive finding above). The threshold and within inputs are copied into five files now that `FlowRunStateTriggerFields` is counted. Small divergences like the `parseInt` one appear because of this copying.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-queue-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="20-46"}

Two extractions would remove most of the duplication and make the posture-to-field mapping consistent by construction:
- a `StatusTriggerFields({ statuses })` component;
- a shared `ThresholdWithinFields` component.
